version: "2.0"
description: >-
  Gnomon signed platform helper (docs/specs/gnomon.md §3.1, §16, §18). npm
  shell (private) holding the built, signed binaries in bin/ (gitignored build
  output) with checksums.json (advisory). The kernel verifies the helper at
  spawn (G-HLP-01): code-signing requirement + version floor, and in a
  release build the sha256 that packages/gnomon's release manifest pins
  (#release-pins, T-2026-10-21-028).
  darwin/ is a SwiftPM package (swift-tools 5.9, macOS 14); scripts/ builds
  the signed .app (Developer ID identity looked up in the keychain, the
  provisioning profile embedded from ~/.config/gnomon-signing) and optionally
  notarizes it (keychain profile gnomon-notary). win32/ is the Rust helper
  (P8-D71, W2 of docs/plans/gnomon-windows.md), built by scripts/build-win32.mjs.

components:
  gnomon-helper:
    description: >-
      Holds what the kernel must never hold. M1.3 slice 1: the root and
      approvals P-256 keys in the Secure Enclave (access group
      SW4BMQ8PNL.gnomon, .privateKeyUsage + .userPresence), a sign API over
      stdin/stdout (`serve` NDJSON loop, `once` for one request) speaking
      gnomon-helper:v1 — info, key.create/public/delete, sign (root: fresh
      presence per signature; approvals: fresh, or a per-connection cached
      unlock held here keyed by the request's connId string, bounded to 32,
      dropped 12 h after it opened or after 512 signatures under it, with no
      separate idle limit (TD-2026-10-20-086 F4, helper 1.7.0; 1.6.0 was
      15 idle min / 60 min / 64; a kept fresh sign reports `bounds` so the
      broker opens the same caps, else it keeps the 1.6.0 ones; F5: the
      kernel sends the GUI device's `udev:` key there so the unlock survives
      a reconnect; UnlockBoundsTests.swift), on unlock.drop and on exit),
      unlock.drop(-all). Every fresh sign (root and approvals) returns
      `shown`, the helper's own prompt line, which the provisioner checks names
      the digest it displayed (helper 1.5.0; 1.4.0 omitted it for root, so
      guided setup failed helper-line-mismatch). M4: promptText strips every format (Cf), control
      and line/paragraph separator from the caller text before the OS prompt. Each key signs
      only its own domain prefixes (root: row-sig, presence; approvals:
      approve, presence). Inputs bounded before any key is touched. Refuses
      to run sandboxed (P8-D68). Never prints key material. Helper 1.6.0
      (protocol gnomon-helper:v2, V5 of TD-2026-10-19-194, spec §14.7,
      S29-K5, G-BRK-06): voice ARMS in their own store (Custody.swift, cap
      Bounds.maxArms 8, separate from maxUnlocks 32), opened by arm.open
      (fresh presence bound to {humanConnId, voiceConnId}); arm.sign signs
      ONLY gnomon:approve-voice:v1 for that pair, and neither fresh nor
      cached signing ever accepts that domain; arm.close(-all); conn.close
      drops a connId's unlock and every arm naming it. SessionWatch.swift
      observes screen lock, screensaver, display sleep, system sleep and
      user switch (distributed + NSWorkspace notifications, IOKit power, a
      CGSession poll at a 250 ms cadence), invalidates every arm and unlock, bumps the
      epoch (a prompt open across the event is never kept), then pushes an
      unsolicited {push:'session', cause, arms, unlocks, seq} line. serve
      runs requests on a reader thread and the main run loop for the
      observer; Output serializes stdout. Aegis V5 fixes: arm.sign parses the
      frozen gnomon:voice-decision:v1 preimage (gnomon-contracts
      voice-decision.ts) and refuses unless it names this arm and pair;
      arm.open and arm.sign fail closed on a locked, off-console or unknown
      session (CGSessionProbe); maxSignatures required (ceiling 1024); the
      helper arm line leads the prompt, caller text capped at 120. Helper
      1.8.0 (TD-2026-10-20-514, G-ID-09, G-ID-10, Aegis C1-C4, C7):
      decision.sign — ONE fresh evaluatePresence signs a card decision's
      approvals messages (approve:v1 + presence:v1, at most one each) and
      every root row (1..8, the gnomon-root-decision:v2 row first) under the
      same context, invalidated at once; never keep, never Unlocks.put; 10
      messages and 256 KiB at most. bindDecisionBatch checks the decision
      row against the approve message, sha256(intent) against rowsDigest,
      each later row (volatile fields dropped) against intent[i], cardId and
      manifestHash binding, and the closed per-cardKind row table;
      Helper 1.9.0 (TD-2026-10-22-769): the `local.config` row table (ONE
      gnomon-root-local:v1 row: closed fields, 1..4 loopback endpoints with a
      local compat, a pinned runner; localRowRefusal/localRowShown, mirrored
      in the kernel's decision-batch.ts and held to the shared vectors) and
      `requestedBy` in the loop.trusted field set; regexes are ASCII-only and
      must span the whole string (fullMatch), so ICU and JS agree. The kernel
      refuses a loop-trust or local.config card at raise on an older helper;
      Aegis review: the local row's runner is never compacted (dir and node
      <= 256 chars, too-large-to-show otherwise), runnerSource bundled |
      override is in the closed row and an override is marked on the line;
      loop.trusted by ('root' or a principal) and requestedBy are principal
      ids of at most 128 chars;
      Helper 1.11.0 (TD-2026-10-29-746/-894/-340, G-HLP-03, G-HLP-04): the
      caller check (Caller.swift: LOCAL_PEERTOKEN on stdin, the unchanged
      parent, the hardcoded gnomon.kernel requirement with kSecCSStrictValidate,
      hardened runtime, no get-task-allow, never ad-hoc or gnomon.node, the
      SEA's build stamp version floor (1.11.2: read from section __GNOMON_STAMP, the name postject writes for the GNOMON_STAMP resource; 1.11.0-1.11.1 looked for GNOMON_STAMP and refused every SEA kernel as caller-refused: no build stamp); gnomon.kernel.dev only with
      -DGNOMON_HELPER_DEV / build-darwin.mjs --dev-kernel) guards the
      no-presence ops: kernel.cert (SE kernel-cert key, group
      SW4BMQ8PNL.gnomon.prod only, AfterFirstUnlockThisDeviceOnly checked from
      accc; gnomon-kernel-cert:v1 bound to boot, log, pid + pidversion and
      cdhash, 24 h, 4 per boot-hour), anchor.read / anchor.write (gnomon-head,
      rollback/fork refused, 10 writes a second), presence.mode /
      presence.mode.on (the helper's own gnomon-presence-mode item; off only
      after a signed presence.mode decision), click.open / click.sign (a
      non-permanent SE click key for an app-bound connection while the stored
      mode is off; approve:v1 only, never a root card; revoked on close, push,
      12 h and mode-on; 60 a minute, 512 per key). Row tables presence.mode
      and kernel-custody (the cert-key row must name this helper's key).
      Tests: CallerTests, KernelCertTests, AnchorTests, ClickKeyTests and the
      shared vectors;
      Helper 1.10.0 (TD-2026-10-28-969, C6'a2-h, G-HLP-02): the `permission`
      row table — a destructive brokered Always (rootDecisions ['always'],
      rootRows []) signs its gnomon-root-decision:v2 row only; any further
      row is refused batch-refused before a prompt or a key, and the table
      stays closed to every other kind. The kernel offers a destructive
      Always only from this version (ROW_TABLE_MIN_HELPER.permission);
      testPermission* and testAnUnknownKindIsStillRefused in
      DecisionSignTests.swift, plus the shared permission vectors;
      decisionLine names what every row grants and every digest, FIRST in
      the dialog, refused rather than truncated. The TS mirror and shared
      vectors: gnomon src/kernel/identity/decision-batch.ts,
      gnomon-contracts test/fixtures/decision-batch-vectors.json. Root key:
      fresh presence per DECISION. `probe-decision <gen>` is the on-device
      check (one dialog, both keys, an unevaluated context fails). Unit
      tests: darwin/Tests (swift test; DecisionSignTests.swift). Later: the
      kernel-key keystore item, the keystore log anchor, XPC under launchd,
      the Windows pipe, job objects, WFP, launch-token hand-off.
    path: darwin/Sources/gnomon-helper/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, helper, native, swift, paradigm-8, secure-enclave, m1.3]
  gnomon-helper-win32:
    description: >-
      The Windows platform helper (Rust, windows-sys, P8-D71; W2). gnomon-helper.exe
      --version | serve | once speak the gnomon-helper:v2 envelope with the win32
      platform ops: info (userSid), pipe.secure (opens the kernel's
      kernel pipe gnomon-<hex> READ_CONTROL|WRITE_DAC, sets the protected
      current-user-only DACL, reads it back, reports the pipe's server pid; P03
      proved the DACL is pipe-wide and Node cannot adopt a foreign pipe), pipe.read,
      acl.owner-only (file or dir, OICI on a dir) and acl.read (owner-only
      verdicts from the read-back SDDL: owner = user SID, every ACE an allow for
      it), fs.rename-durable (MoveFileExW REPLACE_EXISTING|WRITE_THROUGH),
      boot.id (win:<PrefetchParameters BootId>), shell.open (loopback http(s)
      only, never a URL carrying a launch token). exec -- <abs .exe> args is the
      job-object launcher: it joins a fresh KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE |
      DIE_ON_UNHANDLED_EXCEPTION job BEFORE spawning, so every descendant is
      in the job with no race and killing the launcher kills the tree
      (G-RUN-03); never a .cmd/.bat, never a PATH lookup. Inputs bounded:
      absolute drive paths only (no UNC, device or .. paths), pipe names
      gnomon-<hex>. W4 phase A (approvals.rs): key.create / key.public / sign for
      the APPROVALS key only — a non-exportable P-256 key in the Microsoft Software
      KSP (gnomon-<gen>-approvals, DPAPI per user); sign {mode:'click'} signs
      approve-domain messages only (gnomon:approve:v1, domain-refused otherwise),
      DER over SHA-256; cached refuses no-unlock. W4 phase B (presence.rs, probe
      P02): the PRESENCE key, a separate P-256 key in the Microsoft Passport
      (Windows Hello) KSP, //gnomon.local/<gen>/presence, NgcCacheType
      AUTH_MANDATORY (Hello on creation and on every signature), key.create /
      key.public {key:'presence'}; sign {mode:'fresh', messages:[approve,
      presence]} signs the presence challenge (gnomon:presence:v1 only) under a
      Hello prompt owned by the foreground window FIRST, then the decision with
      the approvals key; a cancel is presence-denied. unlock.drop /
      unlock.drop-all / conn.close are no-ops; the root key and voice arms refuse
      unsupported-op. Version 0.3.0.
      exec is containment for well-behaved trees (partial: WMI/COM/Task Scheduler
      launches are not descendants, Aegis M4). W5 (sessions): sandbox [--desktop]
      [--low] [--session-sid <S-1-9-4174-2026-1022-n>] runs a session under a
      restricted token (restricting SIDs = the user's enabled groups + user +
      GNOMON_SESSION_SID S-1-9-4174-2026-1022 + this session's own SID), Low
      integrity, an explicit user+SYSTEM default DACL (the launcher itself keeps the
      shielded kernel's), a private Low-labelled desktop created with an explicit DACL,
      in a job with UI limits. acl.session places per path the ACEs naming the Gnomon
      SID (an allow may name the session's own SID: `sid`), replacing every explicit
      Gnomon-SID ACE there: deny-read/write/all, deny-replace (no delete/rename/
      re-ACL and no FILE_DELETE_CHILD: the parent of a literal deny), allow-read,
      allow-write, allow-write-keep (allow below, no replace of the root), clear, and
      the labels label-low / label-readable / label-meta (inherit-only no-read-up: the
      dir itself readable for attributes) / label-no-read-up / label-clear; HKCU\ keys
      take write denies (registry masks). A deny adds an OWNER RIGHTS READ_CONTROL-only
      ACE (replaces the owner's implicit WRITE_DAC, grants nothing a session could
      use). process.confine lowers a running child (the GUI transport) to Low in a
      one-process job; process.shield denies the Gnomon SID every access to a process,
      its threads and its token's default DACL (the kernel shields itself). The kernel
      pipe's DACL denies the Gnomon SID; owner-only verdicts accept those denies.
      Unsigned until W7 (Trusted Signing): a dev kernel pins it by sha256, a
      release kernel refuses it. cargo test runs the pure checks.
    path: win32/src/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, helper, native, rust, windows, paradigm-8, w2, w5]
  gnomon-helper-build:
    description: >-
      scripts/build-darwin.mjs (swift build -c release → GnomonHelper.app,
      Info.plist bundle id gnomon.helper, embedded profile, entitlements,
      codesign --options runtime --timestamp with the one Developer ID
      Application identity of team SW4BMQ8PNL found in the keychain, verify
      --strict and against HELPER_REQUIREMENT, sha256 into checksums.json);
      --unsigned for CI. scripts/notarize-darwin.mjs (ditto, notarytool
      submit --keychain-profile gnomon-notary --wait, stapler) is optional and
      founder-run, never run by tests. scripts/build-win32.mjs: cargo build
      --release --locked → bin/win32/gnomon-helper.exe (gitignored), sha256 into
      checksums.json (unsigned).
    path: scripts/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, helper, signing, build, m1.3]
